column2:
[I shall walk,] my god, a road I have never walked;
231	[ ... ] whose [ ... ] my god, I do not know.
232	Let me c[ome ba]ck(?); let me stay safe —
233	[let me see] your [fac]e with a glad heart:
234	[I will build y]ou a house of your delight;
235	[I will se]at you o[n] thrones.

Do [no]t trust, Gilgameš, in your own strength;
250	let your [ey]es be sated with looking; guard yourself.
251	[Let] Enkidu [g]o in front of you:
252	he has seen the [tra]ck; he has walked the road;
253	[he kn]ows the passes of the forest
254	and all the tricks [of H]uwawa.
255	[The one who g]oes in front keeps the companion safe;
256	[the one whose e]yes are wide open — his own self he gua[rds(?).]
257	May Šamaš [let] you reach your triumph;
258	may your eyes be shown the things your mouth has spoken;
259	may he open for you the barred path,
260	unbolt the road for your tread,
261	unbolt the mountain for your foot.
262-263a	May the night bring you a word to gladden you,
263b-264	and Lugalbanda stand by you in your triumph.
265	Attain your triumph like a boy(?):
266	in the river of Huwawa, the one you are set on,
267	wash your feet;
268	at your night-camp dig a well —
269	let the water in your skin be always clean;
270	pour cool water in libation to Šamaš,
271	and keep Lugalbanda, [your go]d, in mind.
